# server: Wake the next hardware message recipient after dequeue.
# https://gitlab.winehq.org/wine/wine/-/merge_requests/12666 state=opened
# Found while running user32:win on OpenBSD, in the cross-process part of test_window_from_point(). The parent process creates a popup window, while a child process creates a transparent static control and buttons inside it. The window parentage automatically attaches their threads' input queues.

An injected click can remain pending behind a WM_WINE_SETCURSOR message addressed to the parent. When the child examines the shared hardware queue, get_hardware_message() signals the parent and defers the child's following mouse messages to preserve input ordering.

The child's message checks can consume its input changed bits while leaving its mouse wake bits set. Its subsequent MsgWaitForMultipleObjects(..., QS_ALLINPUT) waits for a new input notification. Removing the parent's cursor message makes the child's mouse messages eligible, but release_hardware_message() does not notify the child again.

Without another input event, the child's 100 ms wait can therefore expire despite the pending click. This caused two assertions to fail:

```
transparent window didn't get WM_NCHITTEST message
button under static window didn't get WM_LBUTTONUP
```

After removing a hardware message, re-evaluate the remaining messages using the existing recipient lookup. Notify the first eligible recipient in the same input queue through set_queue_bits(), restoring the relevant wake and changed bits. Stop at that recipient even when it is the current thread, so another thread is not notified for input that remains blocked behind the current thread's messages.

On OpenBSD, the child woke when the parent removed its cursor message and received both expected messages. The complete user32:win run finished with no failures.
